{"name":"Dutch Licorice","hex":"#404b56","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dutch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["wife"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Dutch (“treat cocoa with alkali”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dutch"},{"term":"licorice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of species Glycyrrhiza glabra, or sometimes in North America, the related American licorice plant Glycyrrhiza lepidota.","A type of candy  made from that plant's dried root or its extract."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/licorice"}],"entries":[{"title":"Salty liquorice","blocks":[{"text":"Salty liquorice, also known as salmiak liquorice, salmiac liquorice or salmiakki, is a variety of liquorice flavoured with salmiak salt, and is a common confection found in the Nordic countries, Benelux, and northern Germany."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Swedish_salty_liquorice.jpg/330px-Swedish_salty_liquorice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salty_liquorice","wikidata":"Q1410920"},{"title":"Liquorice","blocks":[{"text":"Liquorice or licorice is the common name of Glycyrrhiza glabra, a flowering plant of the bean family Fabaceae, from the root of which a sweet, aromatic flavouring is extracted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Illustration_Glycyrrhiza_glabra0.jpg/330px-Illustration_Glycyrrhiza_glabra0.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquorice","wikidata":"Q257106"},{"title":"Shades of black","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of black, or off-black colors, are colors that differ only slightly from pure black. These colors have a low lightness. From a photometric point of view, a color which differs slightly from black always has low relative luminance. Colors often considered \"shades of black\" include onyx, black olive, charcoal, and jet."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Color_icon_black_new2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_black","wikidata":"Q7460340"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Salmiakpastillen.jpg/500px-Salmiakpastillen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Salmiakpastillen.jpg/1280px-Salmiakpastillen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Salmiakpastillen.jpg/500px-Salmiakpastillen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A pocket tin containing small salmiak liquorice pastilles in the traditional diamond shape. Pastilles are usually of the hard liquorice lozenge variety.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalmiakpastillen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Salmiak_alphabets.jpg/500px-Salmiak_alphabets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Salmiak_alphabets.jpg/1280px-Salmiak_alphabets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Salmiak_alphabets.jpg/500px-Salmiak_alphabets.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Salmiak liquorice candy in the traditional diamond shape. These candies are usually of the soft to medium-soft liquorice variety.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalmiak_alphabets.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Salmiakki_candies.jpg/500px-Salmiakki_candies.jp